// Uncovered-merge alert: raise for review when a change lands on the canonical
// branch of the canonical repository and no release candidate covers it yet.
// MERGED, CANONICAL_REPOSITORY and CANONICAL_BASE are the host's 0/1 facts
// about the pull request being examined; IS_RELEASE_NOTES_PR is 1 for the
// release-note pull request itself, which is excluded because a release note
// describing itself is not coverage; HAS_RELEASE_CANDIDATE is 1 once some
// draft release note mentions this merge.
// REGIME: all of them. This is bookkeeping about a merge that already
// happened, not a forecast about anything.
// CONDITIONS: the host has decided which repository and which base branch are
// canonical before publishing these facts. Both are policy, and a host that
// publishes 1 for every repository turns this rule into noise.
// INVALIDATION: a release candidate picking the merge up. HAS_RELEASE_CANDIDATE
// goes to 1 and the rule stops firing, with no memory of having fired - the
// host owns the follow-up, not this rule.
// SHAPE: 5m; merges arrive when they arrive, and five minutes is fast enough
// that the author is still around to answer.
// NOT aether_release_notes_batch_unaccounted: that one reads the batch-scope
// count and proposes a hold on publication. This one looks at a single merge
// and deliberately only observes - a missing changelog line is a thing to
// notice, not a thing to stop the world over.
// CALIBRATED ON: nothing numeric; every comparison is against a host boolean.
// What does not travel is the canonical repository and base, which are
// per-programme decisions and belong to whoever runs the release.
strategy AetherReleaseNotesUncoveredMerge {

    agent ReleaseDesk

    every 5m {

        if MERGED >= 1 and CANONICAL_REPOSITORY >= 1 and CANONICAL_BASE >= 1 and IS_RELEASE_NOTES_PR <= 0 and HAS_RELEASE_CANDIDATE <= 0 {

            observe()

        }

    }

}
